    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras are leaning heavily into speed and situational volume today, anchored by **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (80.98), who registers as our premier start. Facing Merrill Kelly and his 5.27 xERA, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill boost and his cemented leadoff role, providing the high-upside stolen base potential we prioritize. Similarly, **Junior Caminero** (70.49) offers our strongest power-speed balance today. Despite a daunting matchup against Framber Valdez, **Junior Caminero** projects well in the three-hole, with park factors and lineup placement cushioning the impact of facing a quality southpaw. While **Lars Nootbaar** (58.28) and **Caleb Durbin** (55.77) sit just outside the elite tier, they provide essential depth; **Lars Nootbaar** specifically exploits a favorable platoon dynamic against Kevin Gausman, justifying his start despite the moderate SP challenge.\n\nOur toughest bench decision centered on **Dillon Dingler** (35.83). While he maintains a solid base, the combination of a difficult matchup against Rasmussen and a sub-40 score effectively forced him off the active roster to preserve our strict game caps. We are proceeding cautiously with **Trea Turner** (52.31) and **Kyle Schwarber** (51.53), as both face Logan Gilbert. While their talent floor keeps them in the lineup, Gilbert\u2019s 3.46 xERA tempers expectations, forcing us to lean on their leadoff and two-hole order advantages to bridge the gap. We continue to monitor the status of **Yordan Alvarez**, **Gary Sanchez**, and **Casey Schmitt** to ensure our bench remains ready to rotate in for tomorrow\u2019s slate, keeping an eye on **Josh Smith**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Hector Rodriguez**, **Luis Pena**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Carson Benge** as we navigate the remainder of this high-leverage August week. **Christian Yelich**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, and **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** remain sidelined due to team off-days.",
